#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "1c25c6d9-21b9-59a5-a563-25cb89788286" :question/math-academy-id "e-16706" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2y-2x^2+3xy^2+3xy+1.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}+3xy+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}+3xy+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4y-4 & 4x+6y+3 \\\\ 4x+6y+3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](images/9a/9a89c30b5dcacbee11ea07f93458a61dd674cc8105af6f0cf5d57898b29b9787.png)\n\nStarting with\n![](images/41/41167d8f07938db9bfbeadb4e672e602d6dfd9c5d0b293a55952b5e8ec7a7c13.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4y-4 & 4x+6y+3 \\\\ 4x+6y+3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4(1)-4 & 4(1)+6(1)+3 \\\\ 4(1)+6(1)+3 & 6(1)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4(1)(1)-4(1)+3(1)^{2}+3(1) \\\\ 2(1)^{2}+6(1)(1)+3(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 6-13\\cdot 13}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{169}\\begin{bmatrix}-107 \\\\ -78\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.367 \\\\ 0.538\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203833 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "a5e04738-9b21-56d1-b483-703b82e3379a" :question/math-academy-id "e-16707" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2] \\times [1,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x-6y+9.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf x_1 = \\big[1, \\: 1.5 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3x-6y+9) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3x-6y+9)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](images/ff/ff3b9d25f1fe5ae3a7f784980aa6bce1340ea9186537f097222fbea82a596257.png)\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6(1.5)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3(1)^{2}-3 \\\\ 3(1.5)^{2}-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 9-0\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{54}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.417\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203834 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "e5c61c4c-52a7-52cb-85fe-bc9c3d5e49ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-16708" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times[0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = xy^2+3x^2-xy-x.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[ 1, \\: 1 \\big]^T,$ and compute the components of the first iteration" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}+6x-y-1 \\\\ 2xy-x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+6x-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+6x-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y-1 \\\\ 2y-1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n![](images/dd/ddb94c6d067c419b06cdbec87426e70db6ee07acca7d42c0900ae386f6fe37ae.png)\n\nStarting with ![](images/8b/8bdb55b26b462507d9810675499558be66e7b970d2f9fdb78e708807e617ce8d.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y-1 \\\\ 2y-1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}+6x-y-1 \\\\ 2xy-x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2(1)-1 \\\\ 2(1)-1 & 2(1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2}+6(1)-(1)-1 \\\\ 2(1)(1)-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get ![](images/da/daee26a8f82011d916bf04d7e374a2b8f471602877fe76335f7f1b242ca86b00.png) Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.182 \\\\ 0.909\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9c1114df-3e50-5262-89b7-9f2dfda31093" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203008} {:answer-field/id #uuid "42402f8f-b70b-5b9a-9b06-9c37c04edfca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "896f7585-083a-5599-bbcd-1fe65ab748ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-256515" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[1, 2] \\cdot [- 2, 1]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = 2x^{2}y + 4x^{2} - 3xy - 8x$.\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $x_{0} = [1,-2]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $x_{1} = [2, 0]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$x_{2} = [$\n{{field-1}}\n$]$\n{{field-2}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "7439bc04-cb9f-570a-81fa-d62522bde2f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e11b3fbb-bc5f-5ec5-b462-27e75571abd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-256520" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[- 6,-3] \\cdot [1, 2]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = 2xy^{2} - 6x + 4y^{3} + 7$.\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $x_{0} = [- 6, 1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $x_{1} = [- 3, 2]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$x_{2} = [$\n{{field-1}}\n$]$\n$1.75$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "b752ce65-65dd-5660-834c-feed2d56d700" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203011} {:answer-field/id #uuid "438f0a7a-e28b-5afb-89c3-781a12da3a36" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10a7e7e7-348e-5afa-aa51-5ee70bac5ed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-256534" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[1, 2] \\cdot [1, 2]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = 3xy^{2} + 3x^{2} - 8xy - x$.\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $x_{0} = [1, 1]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$x_{1} = [$\n{{field-1}}\n$]$\n{{field-2}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6eed4d-3207-5871-b732-3aaed9f76d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f6eed4d-3207-5871-b732-3aaed9f76d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569467} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1b8b6be-a31c-573e-bf6e-e966d1666af4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd16fbf2-f5ff-5242-b610-fb61c79ec2b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-257191" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,0]\\times [-2,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=4{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-0.444$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8xy+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy+6x-y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(8xy+6x-y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}-x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8y+6 & 8x-1 \\\\ 8x-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8y+6 & 8x-1 \\\\ 8x-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}8xy+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8y+6 & 8x-1 \\\\ 8x-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}8xy+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8(-1)+6 & 8(-1)-1 \\\\ 8(-1)-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}8(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-(-1)-2 \\\\ 4(-1)^{2}-(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot 0-(-9)\\cdot (-9)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{81}\\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-0.444 \\\\ -1.012\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fae98102-ed44-578d-91b3-534204bfeae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.087" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae98102-ed44-578d-91b3-534204bfeae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.087" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569836} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae66afd-66cd-529a-9898-9254316b1674" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "272bf8c6-2369-5e98-9adb-ebe6db7bece2" :question/math-academy-id "q-257192" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,0]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$0.348$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-2x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4x{y}^{2}+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}y-x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x{y}^{2}+6x-y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x{y}^{2}+6x-y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y-x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6 & 8xy-1 \\\\ 8xy-1 & 4{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6 & 8xy-1 \\\\ 8xy-1 & 4{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4x{y}^{2}+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}y-x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6 & 8xy-1 \\\\ 8xy-1 & 4{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4x{y}^{2}+6x-y-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}y-x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4(0)^{2}+6 & 8(-1)(0)-1 \\\\ 8(-1)(0)-1 & 4(-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4(-1)(0)^{2}+6(-1)-0-2 \\\\ 4(-1)^{2}(0)-(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{23}\\begin{bmatrix}-31 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.348 \\\\ 0.087\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "734cf3d0-74b6-5892-a2b6-f02c20d36503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "734cf3d0-74b6-5892-a2b6-f02c20d36503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569632} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ba5073-2420-5469-9a7f-592d616a8a29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569921} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4500310-e39a-50db-8ce7-fa44301d2af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.340" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4500310-e39a-50db-8ce7-fa44301d2af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.340" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569751} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8aa5d1d-a446-512d-b74d-a1d0ef0175cf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08790f4f-fd5e-5556-9203-49eaf6e8edd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-257195" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-3xy+2x-{y}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-3xy+2x-{y}^{2}-5) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}-3xy+2x-{y}^{2}-5)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4xy+6x-3y+2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x-2y\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+6x-3y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3x-2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+6x-3y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3x-2y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4y+6 & 4x-3 \\\\ 4x-3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4y+6 & 4x-3 \\\\ 4x-3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4xy+6x-3y+2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x-2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4y+6 & 4x-3 \\\\ 4x-3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4xy+6x-3y+2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x-2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4(-1)+6 & 4(-1)-3 \\\\ 4(-1)-3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3(-1)+2 \\\\ 2(-1)^{2}-3(-1)-2(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ -7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-2)-(-7)\\cdot (-7)}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{53}\\begin{bmatrix}43 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-0.189 \\\\ -0.340\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fdd4c5d-ae23-5a86-9387-8837df6a1812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.286" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fdd4c5d-ae23-5a86-9387-8837df6a1812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.286" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569626} :answer-field/id #uuid "3689dae0-eb0a-5015-be27-722e43abe4c1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569865} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "358b80c0-d90b-5e9f-b42a-9bff7ca22db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "358b80c0-d90b-5e9f-b42a-9bff7ca22db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569519} :answer-field/id #uuid "e32003de-1c52-53ff-8dce-b9605e8b1759" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5830491e-19ce-53b6-8fb0-b5e123e740a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-257198" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}+3xy-2.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[0,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}+3xy-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}+3xy-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10xy-4x+2{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4xy+3x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10xy-4x+2{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+4xy+3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(10xy-4x+2{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+4xy+3x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10y-4 & 10x+4y+3 \\\\ 10x+4y+3 & 4x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}10y-4 & 10x+4y+3 \\\\ 10x+4y+3 & 4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}10xy-4x+2{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4xy+3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}10y-4 & 10x+4y+3 \\\\ 10x+4y+3 & 4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}10xy-4x+2{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4xy+3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}10(1)-4 & 10(0)+4(1)+3 \\\\ 10(0)+4(1)+3 & 4(0)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}10(0)(1)-4(0)+2(1)^{2}+3(1) \\\\ 5(0)^{2}+4(0)(1)+3(0)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 0-7\\cdot 7}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ -7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{49}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -35\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.286\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71cba5d6-193f-5c83-907f-4c9e1d767447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71cba5d6-193f-5c83-907f-4c9e1d767447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569631} :answer-field/id #uuid "60d198fc-19c4-5f13-9019-6484adf07ee3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4005aa77-18e1-5073-a669-e2b096c350ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4005aa77-18e1-5073-a669-e2b096c350ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569529} :answer-field/id #uuid "a16d0722-b98a-5c1d-95f0-119e1fc538e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569908} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f27189-6b5e-525a-89f5-ab63d4ef4e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2f27189-6b5e-525a-89f5-ab63d4ef4e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569703} :answer-field/id #uuid "f939bf4e-3667-591e-a976-8f0a98ca8d20" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5acc736b-d0d8-5439-9126-2975fad286b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-257199" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-4x-2y-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[2,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$4$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-4$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-2$\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-4x-2y-2{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}y-4x-2y-2{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2-4y\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-2-4y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-2-4y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4y & 4x \\\\ 4x & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}4y & 4x \\\\ 4x & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2-4y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}4y & 4x \\\\ 4x & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2-4y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4(1) & 4(2) \\\\ 4(2) & -4\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}4(2)(1)-4 \\\\ 2(2)^{2}-2-4(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d1cb510-1818-547a-8900-e0080154ff41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d1cb510-1818-547a-8900-e0080154ff41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569507} :answer-field/id #uuid "002ec56f-2448-5134-85e4-8cbe3fa3a022" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569846} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87cb53ab-6033-5d2e-a880-f4c5d0d8a4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87cb53ab-6033-5d2e-a880-f4c5d0d8a4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569666} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b0c455-67d2-5558-afa0-2900b8085ad6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569888} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d355a1ab-4760-5da8-81c8-ff82042eba6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d355a1ab-4760-5da8-81c8-ff82042eba6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569776} :answer-field/id #uuid "d16c318b-433e-5219-bbac-c0dceacd735f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0e5e71c-da68-5727-a150-890764f120a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-257201" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=4x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-6xy-x.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$10$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-9$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$10$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-6xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-6xy-x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6x-6y-1 \\\\ 8xy-6x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{2}+6x-6y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-6x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{2}+6x-6y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(8xy-6x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8y-6 \\\\ 8y-6 & 8x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8y-6 \\\\ 8y-6 & 8x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6x-6y-1 \\\\ 8xy-6x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8y-6 \\\\ 8y-6 & 8x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}^{2}+6x-6y-1 \\\\ 8xy-6x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8(2)-6 \\\\ 8(2)-6 & 8(1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}4(2)^{2}+6(1)-6(2)-1 \\\\ 8(1)(2)-6(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 8\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c922bd52-0c29-5fbc-bcbf-4f7cdd4dc33a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c922bd52-0c29-5fbc-bcbf-4f7cdd4dc33a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569763} :answer-field/id #uuid "022dd755-e937-5f56-968f-8fdac582d253" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569847} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b52d19c7-00d1-561d-b860-e1f24690955d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b52d19c7-00d1-561d-b860-e1f24690955d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569730} :answer-field/id #uuid "940f4f61-0df6-5fbf-92d5-6c0094f915aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7904745e-3c48-5473-a84d-2ea698eb26ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-257208" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,0]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-3y.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,2]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-0.2,\\,1.2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-3y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-3y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2xy-4x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2xy-4x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy-4x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2y-4 & -2x \\\\ -2x & 6y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-2y-4 & -2x \\\\ -2x & 6y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2xy-4x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-2y-4 & -2x \\\\ -2x & 6y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2xy-4x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 \\\\ 1.2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-2(1.2)-4 & -2(-0.2) \\\\ -2(-0.2) & 6(1.2)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2(-0.2)(1.2)-4(-0.2) \\\\ 3(1.2)^{2}-(-0.2)^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 \\\\ 1.2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-6.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 7.2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1.28 \\\\ 1.28\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 \\\\ 1.2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-6.4)\\cdot 7.2-0.4\\cdot 0.4}\\begin{bmatrix}7.2 & -0.4 \\\\ -0.4 & -6.4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1.28 \\\\ 1.28\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 \\\\ 1.2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{46.24}\\begin{bmatrix}8.704 \\\\ -8.704\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-0.012 \\\\ 1.012\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7379b188-4efc-50fd-a5e6-316fc647872f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7379b188-4efc-50fd-a5e6-316fc647872f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569633} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c0bc89-2005-520a-bca1-7bb0ed5feeab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a610d803-1f33-5c3b-8170-83c03be1f7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a610d803-1f33-5c3b-8170-83c03be1f7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569710} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1efd346-8fdf-5f03-b6fe-b0884388016a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569909} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "552ba9b1-300d-5b6c-941f-998b43f13bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.646" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "552ba9b1-300d-5b6c-941f-998b43f13bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.646" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569567} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d09404-adf2-557f-9a06-f5a9863cc8b7" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569922} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "684dfc30-4dab-50da-adfc-01aa883edaa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "684dfc30-4dab-50da-adfc-01aa883edaa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569605} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ba4597-ac27-5690-bf79-d5368cab1239" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e391a05d-f936-58b5-934a-ad5b4d40ee91" :question/math-academy-id "q-257213" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [-1,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=5x{y}^{2}-2xy-{x}^{2}+3y.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1,\\,-1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration. Round the answer for ${x}_{1}$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-5$\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$10$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-0.524$\n </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x{y}^{2}-2xy-{x}^{2}+3y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x{y}^{2}-2xy-{x}^{2}+3y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{y}^{2}-2y-2x \\\\ 10xy-2x+3\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{2}-2y-2x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(10xy-2x+3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{y}^{2}-2y-2x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(10xy-2x+3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 10y-2 \\\\ 10y-2 & 10x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 10y-2 \\\\ 10y-2 & 10x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}5{y}^{2}-2y-2x \\\\ 10xy-2x+3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 10y-2 \\\\ 10y-2 & 10x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}5{y}^{2}-2y-2x \\\\ 10xy-2x+3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 10(-1)-2 \\\\ 10(-1)-2 & 10(1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}5(-1)^{2}-2(-1)-2(1) \\\\ 10(1)(-1)-2(1)+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}-29/82 \\\\ 39/82\\end{bmatrix}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{29}{82} \\\\ \\frac{39}{82}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{29}{82} \\\\ \\frac{39}{82}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.646 \\\\ -0.524\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00372683-1a17-5683-b8ec-944ce9ac2dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00372683-1a17-5683-b8ec-944ce9ac2dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569446} :answer-field/id #uuid "4723ac7c-15e6-5105-8cff-0c025fd5d952" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569871} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3e6c084-0078-55f2-a9b5-7b352c3ed7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3e6c084-0078-55f2-a9b5-7b352c3ed7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569805} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfb717c0-13de-5925-8e9a-3605309732dc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569919} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce8de537-983e-5c76-847d-0ef82aa8faa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce8de537-983e-5c76-847d-0ef82aa8faa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569769} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1f5f30c-549c-5b34-abb8-940275af359d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569929} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d4c5fbe-8fba-5289-91bc-1a6d34117d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.667" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d4c5fbe-8fba-5289-91bc-1a6d34117d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.667" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569583} :answer-field/id #uuid "f29ded99-6e42-55a8-9e12-efe44e59e72b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce47ce1b-5e64-5fb8-88b0-27f2349480f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-257214" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}-6xy+2x.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[0,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration. Round the answer for ${x}_{1}$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$6$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-2$\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$6$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}-6xy+2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6x{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}-6xy+2x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6{y}^{2}-6x-6y+2 \\\\ 12xy-6x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{y}^{2}-6x-6y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(12xy-6x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{y}^{2}-6x-6y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(12xy-6x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 12y-6 \\\\ 12y-6 & 12x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 12y-6 \\\\ 12y-6 & 12x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}6{y}^{2}-6x-6y+2 \\\\ 12xy-6x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 12y-6 \\\\ 12y-6 & 12x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}6{y}^{2}-6x-6y+2 \\\\ 12xy-6x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 12(1)-6 \\\\ 12(1)-6 & 12(0)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}6(1)^{2}-6(0)-6(1)+2 \\\\ 12(0)(1)-6(0)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1/3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.667\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b968de3-48ac-5509-bda5-bdd182bb0096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b968de3-48ac-5509-bda5-bdd182bb0096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569462} :answer-field/id #uuid "1efb643c-734b-58b8-990d-a15c076650e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5cde4ac-83ff-571c-bb29-6811f5eb0038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5cde4ac-83ff-571c-bb29-6811f5eb0038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569757} :answer-field/id #uuid "51ef4741-c3ce-5d22-a88d-9e3bfc963ad1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569874} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f020a22-0c34-5fb9-b34e-9967438d2085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.612" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f020a22-0c34-5fb9-b34e-9967438d2085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.612" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569670} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a9a83ee-ac72-544e-a5b3-d25e821ddf5c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569904} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60130157-f623-5097-835c-7ab98079c34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60130157-f623-5097-835c-7ab98079c34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569590} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8074289-ced7-55df-b16d-486ca5eaf823" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4033d90f-6820-5b6e-97e2-e268a68a992f" :question/math-academy-id "q-257215" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,0]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}-xy+x-y.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[-1,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration. Round the answer for ${x}_{1}$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-5$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-1$\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-5$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-0.510$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}-xy+x-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}-xy+x-y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-y+1 \\\\ -4xy-x-1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4xy-x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4xy-x-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -4y-1 \\\\ -4y-1 & -4x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -4y-1 \\\\ -4y-1 & -4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-y+1 \\\\ -4xy-x-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -4y-1 \\\\ -4y-1 & -4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-y+1 \\\\ -4xy-x-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6(-1) & -4(1)-1 \\\\ -4(1)-1 & -4(-1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}3(-1)^{2}-2(1)^{2}-1+1 \\\\ -4(-1)(1)-(-1)-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}24/49 \\\\ -19/49\\end{bmatrix}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{24}{49} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{49}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{24}{49} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{49}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-0.510 \\\\ 0.612\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17817482-f3d2-5615-b2cb-6c119b34c2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17817482-f3d2-5615-b2cb-6c119b34c2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569479} :answer-field/id #uuid "5789f198-74e1-5b68-a9ba-3bb1808b8652" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d2da55-b35c-5e37-bc82-32d67ef03d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19d2da55-b35c-5e37-bc82-32d67ef03d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569482} :answer-field/id #uuid "862f5751-3521-5986-9159-683bde1d18c7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "040d983b-9569-5e20-9386-5dd2354e86bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-257222" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{4}+{y}^{4}-4{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}+10.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[2,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}+{y}^{4}-4{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}+10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}+{y}^{4}-4{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}+10)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3}-8x \\\\ 4{y}^{3}-8y\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{3}-8x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3}-8y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{3}-8x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{3}-8y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12{x}^{2}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12{y}^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}12{x}^{2}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12{y}^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3}-8x \\\\ 4{y}^{3}-8y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}12{x}^{2}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12{y}^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3}-8x \\\\ 4{y}^{3}-8y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}12(2)^{2}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12(2)^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4(2)^{3}-8(2) \\\\ 4(2)^{3}-8(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}40 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 40\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{40\\cdot 40-0\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}40 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 40\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{1\\,600}\\begin{bmatrix}640 \\\\ 640\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1.6 \\\\ 1.6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccb984f5-1d1e-5d3d-89ea-171fda9ca2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccb984f5-1d1e-5d3d-89ea-171fda9ca2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569767} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e92ca9-095d-5013-a0f3-2a43f9ecb853" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11575fd3-3b68-5f5f-9c8d-ffa66ae7367b" :question/math-academy-id "q-257248" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[2,3]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-6y+13.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[3,\\,2]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[2.25,\\,1.5]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$1.417$\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-6y+13) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-6y+13)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6x-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6x-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6x-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.25 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6(2.25)-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6(1.5)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3(2.25)^{2}-6(2.25) \\\\ 3(1.5)^{2}-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.25 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}7.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1.6875 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.25 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{7.5\\cdot 9-0\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7.5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1.6875 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.25 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{67.5}\\begin{bmatrix}15.1875 \\\\ 5.625\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}2.025 \\\\ 1.417\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
